  "summary": "Profiling now separates tissue-resident ageing programmes from systemic trends and tests germ layer origin in ageing.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of multi-layered transcriptomic and chromatin profiling to resolve stromal and tissue-resident cell states, and to ask how origin, tissue context and ageing shape them. Paired bulk, single-nucleus RNA and chromatin accessibility data place healthy human synovium and infrapatellar fat pad stromal cells on a continuous state landscape. In human stromal clones, HIPPO competency signatures and oxidative metabolism are linked to bone-forming potential and osteogenic responsiveness. Across ages, mouse cochlear single-nucleus data show weak concordance with systemic ageing, with macrophages following a distinct tissue-resident programme. Bulk profiling of 24 GTEx tissues indicates that germ layer origin leaves a small but detectable imprint on transcriptomic ageing, with shared immune programmes across tissues. Bovine mesenchymal cells from umbilical cord and adipose tissue show shared and context-dependent adaptations across culture systems, relevant to cultivated meat. All five items are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
